    add(id, buy, price, qty);
}
```

An add can only improve a best price, an O(1) compare. A cancel or execute that empties the best level walks to the next live one, usually a tick or two away, but across the whole band when a side empties. The `LevelBitmap` from *Bit Tricks and SIMD* replaces that walk with a few bit scans. Its 64 words and one summary word cover 4096 levels, so this 16384-tick band needs 256 words with a summary of 4 words, or a third level. On 10^6 random adds and cancels built with `// -O2`, the list-and-index book from the previous part took about 43 ms and a million allocations here, and this one about 7.5 ms and none, with the same best prices after every message. Those times are rough, since the sandbox runs x86-64 emulated, but the allocation counts are exact.

